<article class="prose">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
  <h1>Accounts</h1>
  <p class="lede">An account is a place money sits: a chequing account, a card, or something you own that you do not spend from.</p>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>Cash and cards</h2>
      <p>Open <strong>Spending</strong>, then <strong>All Accounts</strong>. A new budget starts with Chequing and Credit Card. <strong>Add Account</strong> asks for a balance and an as-of date.</p>
      <p>Spending on a credit card sets the payment aside in that card’s category. When you pay the card from chequing, that set-aside money is what covers the payment.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img src="/images/quick-start/accounts.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The Accounts list, with Chequing and Credit Card both at zero.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>Tracking accounts</h2>
  <p>A tracking account is for something that belongs in your net worth and not in the spending plan: a house, a car, or investments held outside the budget. For those, the action is <strong>Update Value</strong> rather than Reconcile. A debt you track, such as a loan, still uses Reconcile.</p>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
  <h2>Clear, then reconcile</h2>
  <p>A transaction starts uncleared. The <strong>C</strong> at the end of the row is an outline until you match it to your statement. On iPhone, press and hold the row and tap <strong>Mark Cleared</strong>. On Mac, click the C. A filled C is cleared. A lock means the row is already reconciled.</p>
  <p>Under <strong>Cleared</strong> on the account, tap <strong>Reconcile</strong>. Enter the <strong>Statement balance</strong> as of the statement date. Cleared transactions on or before that date are locked. Uncleared ones are left out. If the statement differs from the cleared balance, ZxBudget posts an adjustment for that day.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/guides/quick-start/">quick start</a> walks through this once, with the same pictures.</p>
    </div>
